INT. COLD-STORAGE FILM VAULT - NIGHT

A refrigerated concrete room lined with steel shelves, each stacked with canisters of nitrate film. Breath fogs. Frost rimes the door seal. A single overhead panel hums. At the far end, a locked steel cabinet marked ARCHIVE REEL 14.

CELESTINE ADEYEMI-BROOKS, 30s, in a thermal jacket and surgical gloves, kneels at the cabinet with a pick set. Beside her, GRAHAM TOLLIVER, 50s, in a parka two sizes too big, holds a flashlight and an empty duffel. Neither has spoken for some time.

She works the pick. A click. Another. She pauses, blows on her fingers, and resumes. He shifts the flashlight to his other hand.

CELESTINE
Hold it steady.

GRAHAM
I am.

CELESTINE
It's shaking.

GRAHAM
It's cold.

She says nothing. The pick turns. Something inside the cabinet gives with a soft, definite clunk. She lifts the latch and swings the door wide. Inside lie a dozen flat tin canisters, labeled in faded ink.

GRAHAM (CONT'D)
That's it?

CELESTINE
That's it.

GRAHAM
I thought it would be bigger.

CELESTINE
It's film.

She lifts the first canister, reads the label, sets it in the duffel. He lifts the second. They work in silence, passing each tin hand to hand. Frost crackles underfoot.

GRAHAM
You know what's on these?

CELESTINE
I know what the buyer told me.

GRAHAM
That's not what I asked.

CELESTINE
Then no.

GRAHAM
Nineteen thirty-four. A premiere in Lisbon. Eleven minutes no one has seen since.

CELESTINE
Eleven minutes isn't worth four hundred thousand.

GRAHAM
It is if it's the right eleven minutes.

She stops. Looks at him. He doesn't meet her eyes.

CELESTINE
You've seen it.

GRAHAM
No.

CELESTINE
You know someone who has.

GRAHAM
My father projected it once.

CELESTINE
Where?

GRAHAM
A basement. Nineteen seventy-one. He said it was the best thing he ever saw and the worst thing he ever did.

CELESTINE
What was on it?

GRAHAM
He never said.

She picks up the next canister. Her hands are steady. His flashlight beam trembles on the label.

CELESTINE
We should go.

GRAHAM
Yes.

They fill the duffel to the top. She zips it. He takes the straps and swings it onto his shoulder. They stand facing each other in the frozen light.

CELESTINE
Graham.

GRAHAM
Hm?

CELESTINE
If we watch it, we can't unwatch it.

GRAHAM
I know.

CELESTINE
If we don't, we'll wonder.

GRAHAM
I know that too.

She nods. She takes the flashlight from him, switches it off, and the vault goes dark except for the humming panel. Then she switches the flashlight back on.

CELESTINE
Fourteen minutes to the van.

GRAHAM
Is that enough time?

CELESTINE
Not for the film.

She turns toward the door. He follows. The frost closes around them, and the cabinet behind them hangs open, empty, glinting.
